 ![A machine's Docker tab: containers, their images, vulnerabilities and status](images/docker.png)
 
+## Every container of the fleet
+
+**Docker → Containers** (`/docker/containers`, `machine.view`) is one
+table of every container on every machine the account can see, read from
+each machine's last monitoring sample - nothing is asked over SSH, so it
+opens at once even for a large fleet. The header shows the **Docker**
+entry once some machine has reported Docker. Its second tab links to
+[Security → Container vulnerabilities](#across-the-fleet-and-accepting-a-vulnerability).
+
+- **Columns**: machine (and its group), container (with its stack and
+  auto-update badge), ports / links, image (with *newer image* when one
+  exists), vulnerability counts of the stored scan, status. Every column
+  but ports sorts with a click on its heading; sorted by machine, a
+  machine's name repeats muted.
+- **Filters** (a plain GET form, so a filtered view can be bookmarked):
+  text (machine, container, image, stack, ports, group), machine group,
+  *critical* or *critical or high* vulnerabilities, *newer image
+  available*, and the state tiles (*Running*, *Unhealthy*, ...). A click
+  on a stack name shows only that stack's containers. The tiles count the
+  rows matching the other filters.
+- **100 rows per page.** For very large fleets the list stops at 20,000
+  containers and says so.
+- **Several containers at once**: tick rows on any machines and *Start* /
+  *Restart* / *Stop* (`action.power`), *Update selected* or *Scan
+  images* (`action.updates`). The action runs machine by machine through
+  exactly the code of each machine's own bulk buttons (same checks,
+  debcontrol's own containers left out, one audit entry per container).
+  *Remove* stays on a machine's own page. A machine whose host key isn't
+  confirmed has no checkboxes.
+- Only machines whose Docker was readable at the last sample are listed.
+
